how similar tasks were structured, I realize the SRD file itself will provide the specific details for each requirement, so referencing them by ID should be sufficient for the stitch agent to understand what needs implementing.\n\nChecking the requirement states, I notice there's a gap between what was completed in the previous task and what's ready now—R10.1 through R13.4 are marked as ready, suggesting some intermediate requirements may already be done. .x listed for srd070 except R10.1 — so R10.1 is the next ready requirement after whatever was last completed.\n\nActually, looking more carefully: the requirement_states for srd070-fmt lists: R10.1, R11.1, R12.1, R13.1, R13.2, R13.3, R13.4. All with status \"ready\". These are the only requirements listed, meaning all other requirements (R1.x through R9.x) are either complete or not tracked.\n\nOK, I'm confident in my plan. Let me write the final output.\n\nThe cmd/fmt/ directory already has implemented code that the stitch agent will need to modify, while cmd/numfmt/ doesn't exist yet and will need to be created from scratch. Now I'm checking the weight constraints: assuming each requirement has a weight of 1, the first task groups R10.1, R11.1, and R12.1 for a total of 3, which fits within the limit of 4. The second task would bundle R13.1 through R13.4 for a total weight of 4, which exactly meets the constraint. I'm reconsidering my approach—should I tackle fmt and numfmt together, or finish fmt completely first before moving to another utility? Given that fmt has 7 remaining requirements needing 2 tasks while numfmt is untouched with 16 requirements, I'll stick with my plan of allocating 2 tasks to fmt and 2 to numfmt, which aligns with the principle of focusing on the earliest incomplete release.\n\nNow I need to verify the file structure for the stitch agent—fmt code is at cmd/fmt/main.go and cmd/fmt/fmt_test.go, while numfmt will need cmd/numfmt/main.go and cmd/numfmt/numfmt_test.go. The binary names follow 